course: dictionary
lesson: 4416
title: Halteres: a fly's balance organs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A fly has only two proper wings, but it still flies very well. Its back pair of wings has shrunk. «True flies, like houseflies, hoverflies and mosquitoes, have only two wings; their back pair has shrunk into tiny knobs called halteres.» The sentence tells you that halteres are the tiny knobs left in place of the back wings.
explain: Use the clues around the word. The knobs are tiny, and they replace wings, so a haltere is a small stand-in for a wing. The sentence also says they are on true flies, so the word names a body part, not an insect or a tool.
explain: Halteres help a fly balance and steer while it flies. Scientists say they work a bit like a living gyroscope, a spinning device that senses turning. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: "the fly's tiny knobs help it balance in the air" still makes sense, so you have understood the word.
fact: A haltere is one of two tiny knob-shaped organs on flies and mosquitoes.
fact: Halteres are in place of a back pair of wings.
fact: Halteres help flies balance and steer while they fly.
fact: «A fly's halteres» means the tiny knobs that belong to a fly.
fact: Most insects have four wings, but true flies have two wings and two halteres.
exercise: «True flies, like houseflies, hoverflies and mosquitoes, have only two wings; their back pair has shrunk into tiny knobs called halteres.» What are halteres? | A kind of fly food | *Tiny knob-shaped organs that help a fly balance | Sharp teeth on the back of a fly | A second pair of big wings
exercise: What do halteres help a fly do? | Hear sounds from far away | Lay eggs faster | Swim under water | *Balance and steer while flying
exercise: «Their back pair has shrunk into tiny knobs.» Find and copy one word from the sentence that means small. | pair | knobs | *tiny | back
test: What do halteres take the place of? | Its mouth | Its eyes | *Its back pair of wings | Its front legs
test: Halteres sense turning, a bit like a living gyroscope. What does this help a fly do? | Turn into a moth | Grow new wings | *Keep steady and steer | Stop flying
exam: Which sentence uses halteres correctly? | Halteres are the sharp mouthparts of a bee | Halteres are a kind of mosquito bite | *The fly's halteres helped it balance in the air | The halteres ate the fly's dinner
